# Cloudflare R2 credentials for feedback screenshots -- one template for both
# environments, because only the bucket differs. Copy it next to itself, once
# per environment you need, and fill it in:
#
#    cp backend/env/env.example backend/env/development.env   # your machine
#    cp backend/env/env.example backend/env/staging.env       # the Pi
#
# Both copies are gitignored. development.env is read at boot by dotenv-rails,
# which config/application.rb points at env/<RAILS_ENV>.env. staging.env is
# read by nothing on your machine: scripts/staging/deploy sends it to
# /srv/wend/secrets.env on the Pi and links the Pi's own
# backend/env/development.env at it, so the deployed Rails reads it the same
# way yours does. That path is outside the deployed working tree, so it
# survives every deploy, and every step of it works without root. Just deploy
# -- there is no separate upload to remember, and no re-provision.
# This file -- the .example -- is committed, so it is the one place the
# variable names are written down. Never put a real value in it.
#
# Leave everything blank and nothing breaks: the backend only selects the R2
# service when R2_BUCKET is set, and otherwise falls back to local disk
# storage. That is the right state for CI and for a fresh clone.
#
# staging.env is parsed by dotenv (and by systemd, on a Pi whose units still
# read it directly), not by bash. Keep every line plain:
#   NAME=value        yes
#   # a comment       yes, and blank lines too
#   export NAME=v     no -- systemd is not a shell (dotenv would take it, but
#                     the same file has to suit both readers)
#   NAME=$(cmd)       no -- no command substitution, no backticks
# The deploy refuses to send a file that breaks any of those rules. dotenv
# reads the same plain form, so write development.env this way too.
#
# Where the values come from: Cloudflare dashboard -> R2 -> Manage API tokens
# -> Create API token, "Object Read & Write", scoped to the wend buckets. The
# full walkthrough is doc/how-to/cloud-storage.md.

# The S3 API endpoint as a full URL, copied whole from the R2 dashboard --
# bucket -> Settings -> S3 API, or the page shown just after a token is
# created. It looks like
#   https://<32 hex characters>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# Paste it rather than assembling it. Cloudflare serves a certificate per
# account and chooses it by hostname, so an account it does not recognise
# refuses the TLS handshake -- which surfaces as "SSL alert number 40" and says
# nothing about which value was wrong. If you have more than one Cloudflare
# account, note that a *user* API token spans all of them, so the account shown
# beside the token is not necessarily the one holding the bucket.
R2_ENDPOINT=

# The R2 API token's access key id and secret. The secret is shown once, when
# the token is created -- if you lost it, roll the token and re-issue. The Pi
# and your laptop can share one token: it is the bucket, below, that keeps
# their objects apart.
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# The one line that differs between the two copies. Each environment has its
# own bucket, so what you upload while poking at the app never lands in the
# bucket staging is showing people:
#   development.env   R2_BUCKET=wend-feedback-localhost
#   staging.env       R2_BUCKET=wend-feedback-staging
# Unset it and that environment falls back to local disk storage -- fine on a
# laptop, but on the Pi that loses uploads whenever
# /srv/wend/app/backend/storage is cleared.
R2_BUCKET=
